    "id": "guide_borrowing-lending-money-campus-friends",
    "title": "Borrowing Money on Campus: Agreements That Protect Trust",
    "summary": "How to borrow and lend money ethically among campus friends, set written repayment terms, and avoid broken relationships and hostel violence.",
    "content": "Your closest coursemate walks into your room on a Tuesday evening.\n\nHis face is strained. He closes the door quietly, sits on your plastic chair, and leans forward:\n\n*\"Bros, abeg, I am in a serious mess. My lodge landlord gave me 48 hours to pay my balance or he will lock my room. Please, lend me ₦25,000. I swear on my life, my uncle is sending my allowance on Friday. I will transfer it back to you on Friday morning without fail.\"*\n\nYou have ₦30,000 in your account—your entire feeding and textbook budget for the next four weeks.\n\nHe is your friend. You study together. You share past questions. \n\nYou transfer the ₦25,000.\n\nFriday arrives. No transfer. \n\nSaturday passes. Silence. \n\nOn Monday, you ask him about the money. He avoids eye contact, speaks irritably, and says: *\"Ah, bros, why are you rushing me? My uncle has not sent it now. Have small patience! You know I cannot run away with your money.\"*\n\nTwo weeks later, the friendship has disintegrated into cold glares, avoided lecture seats, and simmering hostility.\n\nInformal campus lending is a minefield that destroys friendships, fuels physical hostel brawls, and leaves lenders financially stranded.\n\nHere is the objective framework to handle borrowing and lending on campus with absolute integrity and mutual protection.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Fundamental Law of Campus Lending\n\n> **The Golden Law:** Never lend money to a campus friend that you cannot afford to write off as a permanent gift.\n\n| Audit Question | Scenario A: High Risk | Scenario B: Managed Capacity |\n|---|---|---|\n| **\"If they default 100%, will I starve, fall behind on rent, or fail an exam?\"** | **YES (Capital is critical)** | **NO (Discretionary cushion)** |\n| **Recommended Decision** | **Decline Firmly** | **Lend with Written Terms** |\n| **Action Script** | *\"I sympathize with your situation, but 100% of my funds are locked for non-negotiable school fees and rent this week.\"* | Execute a simple written agreement with a fixed repayment date and verified source of funds before transferring a kobo. |\n\nIf losing that money will compromise your semester feeding, exam docket clearance, or health, **lending it is reckless self-sabotage.**\n\nReview campus scam prevention in [7 campus scams targeting broke students looking for cash](/blog/finances/campus-money-scams-students-nigeria/).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Written Micro-Contract Protocol\n\nIf you choose to lend money to a coursemate, remove ambiguity by creating a simple written **WhatsApp Loan Memorandum**:\n\n> ### Loan Confirmation Memo\n>\n> * Date: 26/09/2026\n> * Lender: Itoya David\n> * Borrower: [Friend's Name]\n> * Principal Amount: ₦15,000 (Transferred via OPay)\n> * Repayment Date: Strictly on or before Friday, 10/10/2026\n> * Repayment Source: October Family Allowance Remittance\n> * Agreement: Both parties agree this is a formal debt, not a gift.\n> * **Borrower replies: \"Confirmed and agreed.\"**\n\nA written confirmation in an active WhatsApp chat:\n* Eliminates the psychological drift where borrowers conveniently \"forget\" the exact sum.\n* Establishes a concrete calendar repayment deadline.\n* Provides undisputed written evidence if escalation to mutual friends becomes necessary.\n\nLearn how to manage shared roommate financial dynamics in [splitting hostel utilities fairly without losing friends](/blog/finances/splitting-bills-roommates-fairly/).\n\n---\n\n## 3. How to Decline Loan Requests with Grace\n\nSaying \"no\" to a friend feels uncomfortable, but bad loans destroy friendships far faster than clean boundaries:\n\n### Script A: The \"Pre-Allocated Capital\" Defense\n> *\"Emeka, I value our friendship deeply and I understand how tight things are. However, my remaining balance is pre-allocated for my final semester departmental project binding this week. I don't have surplus liquid funds to lend.\"*\n\n### Script B: The \"Partial Gift Alternative\"\n> *\"Chinedu, I cannot lend you ₦20,000 because I cannot risk my feeding budget. But because you are my brother, take this ₦3,000 as a free gift to sort out food for the next few days. You don't need to pay it back.\"*\n\nGiving a small ₦2,000 or ₦3,000 gift protects your core capital, helps your friend immediately, and carries zero toxic debt overhang.\n\nExplore upcoming analysis of annual rent vs installment options in [paying full lodge rent upfront vs 2-installment plan](/blog/finances/).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Protect Your Integrity as a Borrower\n\nIf you are the one borrowing:\n* Treat repayment deadlines like sacred oaths.\n* If your expected transfer is delayed, **reach out to the lender before they ask you**:\n  > *\"Bros, my family's transfer was delayed until Monday. I want to keep you informed. You will receive your funds the moment it drops.\"*\n* Proactive communication preserves trust, protects your reputation, and ensures you will always find a helping hand in seasons of true need.\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Why do informal loans between university coursemates usually destroy friendships?\nLack of clear written repayment deadlines: borrowers treat informal loans as indefinite gifts, while lenders expect immediate repayment, breeding silent resentment and bitter confrontation.\n\n### What should a simple student written loan agreement contain?\nPrincipal amount borrowed, specific calendar repayment date, clear method of repayment (bank transfer), and the signatures of both parties and one neutral eyewitness.\n\n### What is the 'Golden Rule' of lending money to campus coursemates?\nNever lend an amount you cannot afford to write off as a complete loss. If lending ₦10,000 means you will starve if they default, politely decline or gift them ₦2,000 instead.\n\n### How can you decline a friend's loan request without causing offense?\nUse the 'Committed Capital' decline script: 'I would love to help, but my remaining funds are strictly allocated for my faculty registration dues this Friday. I cannot lend money right now.'",
