    "featuredImage": "/assets/images/blog/changing-department-200-level-university-nigeria.webp",
    "featuredImageAlt": "Changing Course in 200 Level vs Pushing Through - Nigerian university guide",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is the minimum CGPA required for inter-departmental transfer in Nigerian universities?",
        "answer": "Most federal and state universities (such as UNILAG, UI, UNIBEN, and ABU) require a minimum Cumulative Grade Point Average of 3.00 or 3.50 at the end of 100 Level to approve an inter-faculty or inter-departmental transfer."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can you change course in 300 Level in a Nigerian university?",
        "answer": "Almost universally, no. University Senate regulations generally bar inter-faculty and departmental transfers beyond the beginning of 200 Level, as credit units and prerequisite courses cannot be reconciled in the remaining duration."
      },
      {
        "question": "What happens to your 100-level failed courses when you transfer departments?",
        "answer": "If the failed course is not a requirement in your new department, it does not carry over as a graduation requirement, but it remains on your official transcript and impacts your cumulative grade calculation depending on your university's Senate guidelines."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long does inter-departmental transfer processing usually take?",
        "answer": "Administrative clearance through Heads of Department, Faculty Deans, and the Academic Affairs Directorate typically takes between 4 to 12 weeks across Nigerian federal and state institutions."
      }
    ]
