    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Why do Nigerian university lecturers penalize active voice in lab reports?",
        "answer": "Scientific reporting conventions mandate the third-person past passive voice (e.g., '25 ml of hydrochloric acid was titrated' instead of 'I titrated 25 ml'). Using first-person active pronouns ('I' or 'We') signals non-scientific writing and routinely attracts immediate 2-to-3-mark deductions from laboratory demonstrators."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do lab technologists detect 'cooked' or fabricated experimental data?",
        "answer": "Demonstrators identify fabricated data through impossible theoretical yields, zero experimental error margins, or identical titration titers across adjacent lab benches. Fabricated numbers violate physical apparatus tolerances and frequently result in a zero grade for the entire session."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the single fastest way to cut lab report writing time?",
        "answer": "Deploy the Pre-Lab Drafting Protocol: write the Title, Aim, Apparatus, Theory, and Procedure skeleton in your practical logbook before the laboratory session begins. This leaves only data recording, graph plotting, and basic calculations during the session, slashing post-lab writing time by over 70%."
      },
      {
        "question": "What are the two mandatory precautions required in every physics and chemistry lab report?",
        "answer": "Every report requires apparatus-specific precautions: in optics and titration, 'Parallax error was avoided by viewing the meniscus or scale perpendicularly'; in electrical experiments, 'Key was removed when readings were not being taken to prevent battery drainage and overheating'."
      }
    ]
