    "title": "Renewing Current Lodge vs Moving to New Compound: Signs",
    "summary": "Should you renew your student lodge rent or pack out to a new compound? Evaluate landlord extortion, water collapse, noisy neighbors, and moving logistics.",
    "content": "As the academic session draws to a close, a familiar white slip of paper slips under your lodge door:\n\n*\"Notice of Rent Review and Renewal for the Upcoming Academic Session.\"*\n\nYou open it to discover that your landlord has increased your rent by **₦80,000**, added a new *\"Compound Maintenance Fee\"*, and expects full payment within 14 days of resumption.\n\nInstantly, the debate ignites:\n* Should you pay the increased rent and renew your lease?\n* Or should you pack your boxes, hire a logistics mini-truck, and move to a new compound?\n\nMoving is exhausting, expensive, and risky. But staying in an unlivable compound destroys your academic peace. Here is the strategic 5-point audit to decide whether to renew or relocate.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Moving Sunk-Cost Audit: Calculate the \"Exit Tax\"\n\nUndergraduates often assume moving to a lodge priced ₦40,000 cheaper will save money. They forget the **relocation friction costs**:\n\n* **New Agent Registration & Inspection Fee:** ₦5,000 – ₦10,000\n* **New Agent Commission (10%):** ₦30,000 – ₦45,000\n* **New Legal/Agreement Fee (10%):** ₦30,000 – ₦45,000\n* **New Caution Deposit:** ₦25,000 – ₦40,000\n* **Logistics Truck / Keke Moving Trips:** ₦15,000 – ₦25,000\n* **Room Cleaning, Minor Plumbing, and Repainting:** ₦15,000\n* **Total Moving Friction Expense:** **₦120,000 – ₦180,000**.\n\nIf your current landlord's rent increment is ₦50,000, paying the increment is **₦70,000 cheaper** than moving to a new lodge with identical base rent.\n\nOnly move if the new compound delivers massive functional improvements that justify the moving expense.\n\nLearn how to negotiate rent increases calmly in our guide on [dealing with sudden student lodge rent hikes](/blog/hostel-and-accommodation/student-lodge-rent-increase/).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 4 Non-Negotiable Signs You MUST Move Out\n\nThere are situations where paying any moving fee is infinitely better than staying:\n\n1. **Security Vulnerability:** If your compound was burgled during the session and the landlord refused to install security razor wire, iron gate deadbolts, or hire night guards, your life is in danger. Move immediately.\n2. **Permanent Water Collapse:** If the borehole pump has been dead for two months and you spend 90 minutes hauling buckets from neighbouring wells before every 8:00 AM class, your academic stamina is being depleted.\n3. **Chronic Toxic Neighbors:** If adjacent rooms have been rented to disruptive non-students, constant partygoers, or hostile individuals who threaten your safety, no degree of quiet study is possible.\n4. **Predatory Landlord Retaliation:** If the caretaker locks compound water taps or cuts electricity lines whenever tenants raise legitimate maintenance grievances, the relationship is irrecoverable.\n\nReview the signs of bad tenancy in [6 signs your off-campus landlord is exploitative](/blog/hostel-and-accommodation/exploitative-student-lodge-landlord/).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The 30-Day Tactical Relocation Playbook\n\nIf you decide to pack out, execute your exit with strategic precision:\n\n* **Inspect in June/July, Not October:** Never wait until resumption week when thousands of freshers arrive in town and drive up lodge prices. Scout apartments during the final-year clearance window when prime rooms become vacant.\n* **Secure Your Caution Deposit Early:** Request an official room inspection from your caretaker three weeks before departure to document that walls and plumbing are intact, ensuring your deposit is refunded.\n* **Test Utilities at Night:** Before paying for the new room, visit at 8:30 PM. Verify that the compound gate is securely locked, the access street is illuminated, and the borehole pumps clean, odor-free water.\n\nCompare mini-flat options with friends in [self-contained apartment vs room-and-parlour flat](/blog/hostel-and-accommodation/self-contain-vs-room-and-parlour-student/).\n\n---\n\n> ### 🏠 Join the Student Accommodation & Safety Network\n> Thinking of moving compounds next session?\n> \n> Join our **Free Academic Comeback WhatsApp Community**. Connect with verified students from your campus, share genuine lodge reviews, and avoid rogue agents.\n> \n> 👉 **[Join the Student WhatsApp Community Now](https://chat.whatsapp.com/invite/academic-pack-community)**\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### When should you start scouting for a new lodge if you plan to move out?\nStart inspecting new compounds 6 to 8 weeks before the end of the second semester. Graduating final-year students vacate early, giving you first access to prime rooms before the fresher rush.\n\n### What is the average financial cost of moving between student lodges in Nigeria?\nMoving costs roughly ₦80,000 to ₦140,000, including new agent and agreement fees (15-20%), new caution deposit, room repainting, and truck/tricycle moving logistics.\n\n### What is the single biggest sign that you must move out of your current lodge?\nCompromised security (repeated compound break-ins without landlord response) or permanent amenity collapse (borehole pump broken for over two months, forcing daily water hauling).\n\n### Can a landlord arbitrarily increase student lodge rent by 50% upon renewal?\nWhile state tenancy laws regulate arbitrary rent hikes, off-campus student towns operate with lax legal enforcement. Tenants must negotiate collectively or prepare to relocate.",
